 "requested_at": "2026-11-11T15:58:40.584184Z",
 "text": "The clause is enforceable. It doesn't waive or reduce any right the tenant has under the Act, so the void-term rule in **section 14** of the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006* does not apply.\n\nSection 14 voids a tenancy agreement term to the extent it purports to waive or modify a tenant's rights under the Act. This clause does the opposite. Section 34 makes a tenant responsible for undue damage caused by the tenant, another occupant, or a guest. The clause restates that responsibility for damage from a dog, but limits it to damage caused by the tenant's carelessness. Liability that is narrower than the statutory default works in the tenant's favour, so it is not void.\n\nTwo caveats:\n- The clause still requires the landlord to prove both that the damage is undue and that the tenant was careless, which may be hard to establish in practice.\n- I am citing these sections from memory. Check the current text of sections 14 and 34 on e-Laws (ontario.ca/laws) before relying on them.\n\nVerdict: enforceable\nSection: 14",
